            {
              "id": "CVE-2026-6321",
              "name": "LanguageSpecificPackageVulnerability",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "fast-uri: fast-uri: Path traversal vulnerability allows bypass of security policies"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "fast-uri decoded percent-encoded path separators and dot segments before applying dot-segment removal in its normalize() and equal() functions. Encoded path data was treated like real slashes and parent-directory references, so distinct URIs could collapse onto the same normalized path. Applications that normalize or compare attacker-controlled URLs to enforce path-based policy can be bypassed, with a path that appears confined under an allowed prefix normalizing to a different location. Versions \u003c= 3.1.0 are affected. Update to 3.1.1 or later."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "helpUri": "https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-6321",
              "help": {
                "text": "Vulnerability CVE-2026-6321\nSeverity: HIGH\nPackage: fast-uri\nFixed Version: 3.1.1\nLink: [CVE-2026-6321](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-6321)\nfast-uri decoded percent-encoded path separators and dot segments before applying dot-segment removal in its normalize() and equal() functions. Encoded path data was treated like real slashes and parent-directory references, so distinct URIs could collapse onto the same normalized path. Applications that normalize or compare attacker-controlled URLs to enforce path-based policy can be bypassed, with a path that appears confined under an allowed prefix normalizing to a different location. Versions \u003c= 3.1.0 are affected. Update to 3.1.1 or later.",
                "markdown": "**Vulnerability CVE-2026-6321**\n| Severity | Package | Fixed Version | Link |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|HIGH|fast-uri|3.1.1|[CVE-2026-6321](https://avd.aquasec.com/nvd/cve-2026-6321)|\n\nfast-uri decoded percent-encoded path separators and dot segments before applying dot-segment removal in its normalize() and equal() functions. Encoded path data was treated like real slashes and parent-directory references, so distinct URIs could collapse onto the same normalized path. Applications that normalize or compare attacker-controlled URLs to enforce path-based policy can be bypassed, with a path that appears confined under an allowed prefix normalizing to a different location. Versions \u003c= 3.1.0 are affected. Update to 3.1.1 or later."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cvssv3_baseScore": 7.5,
                "cvssv3_vector": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:H/A:N",
